&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;&quot;&gt;Imprisonment is a complex and difficult situation. Deprivation of needs, the necessity to adapt to the imposed rules, and the impact of the prison subculture are common, stressful factors in prisons settings. Tn this study, the influence of chosen gppąlity-based factors (such as psychological resilience, location of control and strategies) on the level ofperceived stress amongpeople serving a prison sentence was examined. The research also took into account the length of punishment as an additional non-personality factor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11.6667px;&quot;&gt;The research&lt;/span&gt; group consisted of 101 oflenders from the prison. Ali psychological wariablęs were measured using self-reports: Feeling of Stress Questionnaire (KPS), Psyhological Resilience Scalę (SPP-25), Tntemal-Extemal Scalę, and brief version of COPE Questionnaire (Mini-Cope).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;&quot;&gt;Resilience and the locus of control turned out to be moderately predictors of perceived stress in inmates. No relationship between preferred coping strategy and the level of stress was found. However, there was a relationship between preferred coping strategy and the length of sentence and the level of psychological resilience. The total duration of imprisonment did not differentiate participants in LOC and perceveid stress.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
